:45,"pro_skipped":true,"finish_details":{"type":"skipped","reason":"user_skipped"},"is_fallback":true},"recipient":"all","channel":null},"parent":"622fdddf-26bc-4034-acc5-20f047d084b7","children":["a08e5e9a-b94f-441d-8239-78fc789780a6"]},"a08e5e9a-b94f-441d-8239-78fc789780a6":{"id":"a08e5e9a-b94f-441d-8239-78fc789780a6","message":{"id":"a08e5e9a-b94f-441d-8239-78fc789780a6","author":{"role":"system","name":null,"metadata":{}},"create_time":1764754556.388786,"update_time":null,"content":{"content_type":"text","parts":[""]},"status":"finished_successfully","end_turn":null,"weight":0,"metadata":{"is_visually_hidden_from_conversation":true,"async_task_id":"promode_6930047c002c81918d0774586a2a344b","async_task_type":"pro_mode","request_id":"2fd3537c-5d3c-48bc-8de4-d4eee8ec6797","message_type":"next","model_slug":"gpt-5-1-pro","default_model_slug":"gpt-5-1-pro","parent_id":"38ff521a-1c19-42b7-bd00-8cf05e1a8931","turn_exchange_id":"813ba244-bb27-4855-b84b-c604254464ab"},"recipient":"all","channel":null},"parent":"38ff521a-1c19-42b7-bd00-8cf05e1a8931","children":["e45b88cd-ac6d-45c0-9c2f-0579b0c9510b"]},"e45b88cd-ac6d-45c0-9c2f-0579b0c9510b":{"id":"e45b88cd-ac6d-45c0-9c2f-0579b0c9510b","message":{"id":"e45b88cd-ac6d-45c0-9c2f-0579b0c9510b","author":{"role":"assistant","name":null,"metadata":{}},"create_time":1764754601.730392,"update_time":1764754599.941745,"content":{"content_type":"text","parts":["What you’re describing is **very common after COVID** and fits with a condition called **parosmia**, a type of smell distortion that can happen when the olfactory system is healing.\n\nHere’s what’s likely going on:\n\n## 🧠 **Why onions smell “wrong” now**\nCOVID can damage the **olfactory receptor neurons** in the nose. As they regrow, the brain can receive *distorted* or incomplete smell signals. Foods like **onions, garlic, coffee, meat, and chocolate** often become the most distorted because their normal smell is made of many complex molecules.  \nWhen the brain gets scrambled or partial input, it tries to interpret it—often resulting in a smell that is:\n\n- burnt  \n- chemical  \n- rotten  \n- overly sweet  \n- like onions or sulfur  \n\nThis is why your **onion smell changed**.\n\n## 💦 **Why your sweat smells like onions**\nThis is another parosmia effect. Your sweat probably *doesn’t actually smell different*, but your **brain is interpreting the odor as “onion-like.”**  \nSome people also experience **phantosmia**—smelling something that isn’t there—especially onion, smoke, or chemical smells.\n\n## ✔️ Is this normal?\nYes—post-COVID smell distortion is extremely common. Studies show:\n\n- Up to **40–50%** of people with COVID-related smell loss develop parosmia as they recover.\n- It can last **months to years**, but many people improve with time.\n\n## 🧪 What actually causes it (in simple terms)\n- COVID injures olfactory neurons.\n- They regrow, but their wiring can be “misconnected” at first.\n- The brain receives mismatched signals → smell distortion.\n\nIt’s similar to hearing static when a radio is partly tuned.\n\n## 🩺 When to get checked\nSee an ENT (ear, nose, throat doctor) if you have:\n\n- symptoms getting *worse* after a year  \n- constant phantom smells (burning, smoke)  \n- sinus issues, blockage, or infections  \n- severe impact on eating or weight loss  \n\n## 👍 Things that may help\n- **Smell training** (clinically recommended): sniffing 4 scents twice daily  \n- **Good hydration + nasal hygiene** (saline rinses)  \n- **Avoid trigger foods temporarily**  \n- Some ENTs may suggest omega‑3s or topical steroids, but this requires medical guidance\n\n---\n\nIf you want, I can walk you through simple smell‑training steps—it’s one of the few things proven to help recovery."]},"status":"finished_successfully","end_turn":true,"weight":1,"metadata":{"sonic_classification_result":{"latency_ms":null,"simple_search_prob":null,"complex_search_prob":null,"no_search_prob":null,"search_complexity_decision":null,"search_decision